 ## Overview
-The Deep Implicit Network (DIN) Regularizer, also known as the Self-supervised Dual Contouring Regularizer (SDR), is a mesh-based training objective for Deep Implicit Networks (DINs) that ensures the predicted Signed Distance Field (SDF) is geometrically consistent with the mesh extracted via Self-Supervised Dual Contouring (SDC) [S1]. Unlike standard DIN training which minimizes error against ground-truth SDF values at query points, this method minimizes the discrepancy between the predicted field and the actual distances to the extracted mesh, thereby resolving ambiguous level sets and improving surface fidelity [S1]. It is distinct from direct SDF supervision by using the extracted mesh $\mathcal{M}$ as the geometric target rather than external ground-truth labels [S1].
+The Deep Implicit Network (DIN) Regularizer, also known as the Self-supervised Dual Contouring Regularizer (SDR), is a mesh-based training objective for Deep Implicit Networks (DINs) that ensures the predicted Signed Distance Field (SDF) is geometrically consistent with the mesh extracted via Self-Supervised Dual Contouring (SDC) [S1]. Unlike standard DIN training which minimizes error against ground-truth SDF values at query points, this method minimizes the discrepancy between the predicted field and the actual distances to the extracted mesh, thereby resolving ambiguous level sets and improving surface fidelity [S1]. It is distinct from direct SDF supervision by using the extracted mesh $\mathcal{M}$ as the geometric target rather than external ground-truth labels [S1]. The method remains self-supervised regarding the mesh target, as it does not require ground-truth mesh data for the regularization term, though it still relies on ground-truth SDF values for the primary training loss [S1].
 
